        "question": {
          ":question/id": "6542b122-54a3-51cc-99a6-84131861886c",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "![](/tmp/ca9837-templates-z7ii9jxi/17592186172088/field-19c21ff476ed9050eb095af214d4495ace544e9253be593b527fe5a323aeb90f.png)\n\nCompare the arrows with the positive directions of the labeled axes. All listed formulas are constant vector fields. Which of the following could generate the field shown?\n\nArrow lengths use a common display scale; no numerical magnitude is specified.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The arrows are parallel to the $y$-axis and point in its **positive** direction. They have the same nonzero length throughout the diagram. A matching field must therefore have zero components on the other axes and a constant positive $\\mathbf j$ component.\n\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf j$ points in the opposite direction.\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y,z)=\\mathbf k$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf K(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf k$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y,z)=\\mathbf i$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n\nTherefore every listed formula is ruled out. The correct response is **None of the listed**.",
