          ":question/worked-solution": "The arrows are parallel to the $y$-axis and point in its **positive** direction. They have the same nonzero length throughout the diagram. A matching field must therefore have zero components on the other axes and a constant positive $\\mathbf j$ component.\n\n- $\\mathbf K(x,y,z)=e^{y}\\,\\mathbf j$ varies in magnitude as $y$ changes; the displayed arrows instead have equal lengths.\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf j$ points in the opposite direction.\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y,z)=y\\,\\mathbf j$ varies as $y$ changes and is zero on $y=0$; the diagram has nonzero arrows of equal length there as well.\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=y^{2}\\,\\mathbf j$ varies as $y$ changes and is zero on $y=0$; the diagram has nonzero arrows of equal length there as well.\n\nTherefore every listed formula is ruled out. The correct response is **None of the listed**.",
